{"type":"message","id":"5ab6a5eb","parentId":"40521863","timestamp":"2026-07-28T12:43:27.883Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Actually, wait. Let me re-examine the cron message: `[cron:c81dcde7-569e-4f24-b00a-73ea1fd815e0 feed-drain-memecoinist-20260728-123714] FEED_DRAIN {\"project\": \"memecoinist\"}`\n\nThe cron job name is `feed-drain-memecoinist-20260728-123714`. This suggests the drain process itself creates the feed jobs, not finds them. Let me look at dispatch_feed_jobs.py again - it says each cron processes exactly ONE story. Maybe I need to look at the actual mechanism...\n\nActually, I think the issue is that the feed drain should fetch headlines from RSS feeds first, THEN process them. Let me check if there's a separate feed fetch process:\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_05fdfaa9cc4647e595530752","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"ls -la ~/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator/skills/pipeline/ | grep -i fetch"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bedrock-mantle","model":"deepseek.v3.2","usage":{"input":25317,"output":242,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":25559,"cost":{"input":0.01620288,"output":0.00046222,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0166651}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1785242599991,"responseId":"chatcmpl-ccb32cf7-2143-4f25-a408-4ad39017529b"}}
{"type":"message","id":"0b9c5c7a","parentId":"5ab6a5eb","timestamp":"2026-07-28T12:43:28.105Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_05fdfaa9cc4647e595530752","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"-rw-r--r-- 1 bhard bhard  2750 Jul 16 13:02 emit_feed_fetch_commands.py"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":183,"aggregated":"-rw-r--r-- 1 bhard bhard  2750 Jul 16 13:02 emit_feed_fetch_commands.py","cwd":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1785242608105}}
{"type":"message","id":"232793a4","parentId":"0b9c5c7a","timestamp":"2026-07-28T12:43:37.273Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_2820f673cf3f438ca6d4177c","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"~/.openclaw/workspace-orchestrator/skills/pipeline/emit_feed_fetch_commands.py"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bedrock-mantle","model":"deepseek.v3.2","usage":{"input":25613,"output":68,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":25681,"cost":{"input":0.016392320000000002,"output":0.00012988,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0165222}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1785242608107,"responseId":"chatcmpl-52dd772f-5628-4178-b89f-a69d5b7662d6"}}
{"type":"message","id":"8bdbada0","parentId":"232793a4","timestamp":"2026-07-28T12:43:37.442Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_2820f673cf3f438ca6d4177c","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"emit_feed_fetch_commands.py -- Print one bash `curl` line per RSS feed\nconfigured for the active project.\n\nThis lets the Researcher SOUL stay project-agnostic. Instead of hardcoding\na feed list per site, the Researcher runs:\n\n    eval \"$(python3 .../emit_feed_fetch_commands.py)\"\n\nand the project's feeds get fetched.\n\nUsage:\n    python3 emit_feed_fetch_commands.py [--project <slug>] [--output-dir /tmp/feeds]\n\nEach emitted line writes the feed body to /tmp/feeds/<slot>.xml (slot = 1..N).\nThe `Source-Name: <name>` header field is preserved in a sidecar\n/tmp/feeds/<slot>.source so the parser can map back to the friendly source name.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport os\nimport shlex\nimport sys\n\nHERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))\nsys.path.insert(0, HERE)\n\nimport project_config as pc  # noqa: E402\n\nDEFAULT_USER_AGENT = \"Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; OpenClawScout/1.0)\"\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    parser.add_argument(\"--project\", default=None)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--output-dir\", default=\"/tmp/feeds\")\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--user-agent\",\n        default=DEFAULT_USER_AGENT,\n        help=\"User-Agent header sent for each curl\",\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--timeout\",\n        type=int,\n        default=12,\n        help=\"Per-request timeout in seconds\",\n    )\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    try:\n        cfg = pc.load_project_config(slug=args.project)\n    except (FileNotFoundError, ValueError) as e:\n        print(f\"# FEED_EMIT_ERROR: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n\n    feeds = cfg.get_path(\"research.rss_feeds\", []) or []\n    if not isinstance(feeds, list) or not feeds:\n        print(f\"# FEED_EMIT_ERROR: no rss_feeds for project {cfg.slug}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 1\n\n    print(f\"# project={cfg.slug} feeds={len(feeds)} output_dir={args.output_dir}\")\n    print(f\"mkdir -p {shlex.quote(args.output_dir)}\")\n    print(f\"UA={shlex.quote(args.user_agent)}\")\n\n    for i, feed in enumerate(feeds, 1):\n        if not isinstance(feed, dict):\n            continue\n        url = str(feed.get(\"url\") or \"\").strip()\n        if not url:\n            continue\n        source = str(feed.get(\"source\") or \"Unknown\").strip() or \"Unknown\"\n        out_xml = f\"{args.output_dir}/{i:02d}.xml\"\n        out_source = f\"{args.output_dir}/{i:02d}.source\"\n        print(f\"# {i}: {source}\")\n        print(\n            f\"curl -sL -A \\\"$UA\\\" \"\n            f\"{shlex.quote(url)} \"\n            f\"--max-time {int(args.timeout)} \"\n            f\"-o {shlex.quote(out_xml)}\"\n        )\n        print(f\"printf %s {shlex.quote(source)} > {shlex.quote(out_source)}\")\n\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1785242617442}}
{"type":"message","id":"50b825c8","parentId":"8bdbada0","timestamp":"2026-07-28T12:43:43.688Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"OK, so there's a feed fetching mechanism. But I think the FEED_DRAIN process is meant to work with already-fetched headlines that are in some queue. Since the database is empty, there's nothing to process.\n\nActually, looking back at the dispatch_feed_jobs.py, it says it \"reclaims stale running jobs, then for each project with queued work and no live drainer lease, fires ONE isolated FEED_DRAIN cron. Each cron processes exactly ONE story.\"\n\nSo the cron should process ONE story if there's queued work. But there's no queued work.\n\nI think I should just exit gracefully since there's nothing to do. Let me send a notification to the Telegram group about the empty queue:\n\n<｜DSML｜function_calls"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f84531cc8a3f48e89c73d97d","name":"message","arguments":{"action":"send","target":"-1004488104862","message":"[FEED_DRAIN] Memecoinist feed drain queue empty. No stories to process."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"bedrock-mantle","model":"deepseek.v3.2","usage":{"input":26478,"output":251,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":26729,"cost":{"input":0.01694592,"output":0.00047941,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.01742533}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1785242617443,"responseId":"chatcmpl-4c20f371-39b3-47b8-90fa-a199a6adad2e"}}
